OSKit PM Server — Process Manager
==================================

Minix 3-style process manager. Handles process lifecycle:
exit notifications, waitpid, kill, and zombie reaping.

The kernel notifies PM when a process exits (via notification).
PM tracks zombies and matches them to waiting parents. When a
parent calls waitpid, PM either returns immediately (if child
is already zombie) or blocks the parent until the child exits.

PM runs in kernel address space and calls reap_process()
directly to free process page tables.

IPC commands:
- PM_WAITPID (1): [cmd(1), pid(4)] → [status(1), exit_code(4)]
- PM_KILL    (2): [cmd(1), pid(4)] → [status(1)]

    module oskit.servers

    import oskit.ipc.{port_create, port_register, port_lookup, ipc_recv_notify, ipc_reply, ipc_send, ipc_last_msg_len}
    import oskit.config.{MAX_PROCESSES, MAX_WAITERS, PROC_ZOMBIE, PROC_FREE, PROC_RUNNING, PAGE_SIZE}
    import oskit.services.{putc, notify_read_self, notify_send_to, thread_id, rs_ready, rs_tid, svc_reap_proc, svc_kill_proc, svc_register_pm, svc_get_thread_pid, svc_get_thread_ptbr, svc_create_proc_susp, svc_resume_proc, svc_vm_create_pt, svc_vm_copy_to, svc_proc_state, svc_proc_exit_code, svc_proc_main_tid, svc_proc_parent_tid, svc_proc_set_parent_tid, svc_grant_posix_range, svc_fd_transplant, svc_proc_replace_image_prep, svc_execve_finalize, svc_vma_create, VMA_PROT_READ, VMA_PROT_WRITE, VMA_MAP_ANON, VMA_MAP_PRIVATE}
    import oskit.loader.{load_tof_to_ptbr, load_elf64_to_ptbr, load_elf64_to_ptbr_at_offset}
    import oskit.lib.{elf64_validate, elf64_entry, elf64_phoff, elf64_phentsize, elf64_phnum, elf64_ph_type, elf64_ph_flags, elf64_ph_offset, elf64_ph_vaddr, elf64_ph_filesz, elf64_ph_memsz, elf64_pf_to_vma_prot, elf64_align_down, elf64_align_up, ELF64_EHDR_SIZE, ELF64_PHDR_SIZE, ELF_OK, ELF_PT_LOAD, ELF_PT_INTERP}
    import oskit.fs.{fs_open, fs_read, fs_seek, fs_close, fs_stat}
    import oskit.arch.{PROG_USP, PROG_SSP, PLATFORM_NAME}
    import std.alloc.{malloc, free}

    val PM_CMD_WAITPID = 1
    val PM_CMD_KILL    = 2
    val PM_CMD_SPAWN   = 3
    val PM_CMD_SIGNAL  = 4
    val PM_CMD_SET_FG  = 5
    val PM_CMD_SIGINT  = 6
    val PM_CMD_SPAWN_SUSP    = 7
    val PM_CMD_TRANSPLANT_FD = 8
    val PM_CMD_RESUME        = 9
    val PM_CMD_EXECVE        = 10

Both arches' `vm_create_process_pt` carves out a single 2 MiB
user-accessible region at VA `[0x60000000, 0x60200000)` — the L3
carve-out under L2[256] of L1[1] on aarch64, mirrored on x86 by
the matching PD[256] entry. Anything outside this range walks into
kernel-attribute mappings and faults on EL0 access. For chunk 9
the dynamic-linker workload has to fit *inside* this 2 MiB window
along with the main executable and the user stack; we partition it
explicitly:

```
0x60000000              main exe (PIE, rebased to MAIN_EXE_BASE)
0x60080000..0x60090000  user stack (PROG_USP - 64KB .. PROG_USP)
0x60100000              ld-musl-<arch>.so.1 (rebased to INTERP_BASE)
0x601FFFFF              end of L3 carve-out
```

`MAIN_EXE_BASE` is where `pm_handle_execve` rebases a PIE main
executable's PT_LOAD vaddrs (link-time near 0); the conventional
0x60000000 keeps the layout aligned with the existing static-binary
slix-prog.ld convention. `INTERP_BASE` puts the interpreter 1 MiB
above the main exe — enough room for any reasonable static binary
or PIE main exe up to 512 KiB, and ld-musl itself is around 900 KiB
so it just fits in the upper 1 MiB. Phase 1's master roadmap
invariant 1 (per-process VMA list, dynamic layout) is what allows
this carving to grow in a later chunk; for now the constants
match the carve-out exactly.

    const MAIN_EXE_BASE: i64 = 0x60000000
    const INTERP_BASE:   i64 = 0x60100000

`AT_*` auxv tags. The values are the standard glibc/musl numbers
(see `bits/auxv.h`); they're ABI to the dynamic linker. PM emits
the subset musl's `__init_libc` reads on aarch64 + x86_64. The
rest are intentionally absent — musl tolerates a missing tag and
falls back to internal defaults / `getenv`.

    const AT_NULL     = 0
    const AT_PHDR     = 3
    const AT_PHENT    = 4
    const AT_PHNUM    = 5
    const AT_PAGESZ   = 6
    const AT_BASE     = 7
    const AT_ENTRY    = 9
    const AT_PLATFORM = 15
    const AT_HWCAP    = 16
    const AT_SECURE   = 23
    const AT_RANDOM   = 25

Signal numbers (SLIX-native, not POSIX).

    val SIG_TERM  = 1
    val SIG_KILL  = 2
    val SIG_CHILD = 3
    val SIG_INT   = 4
    val SIG_PIPE  = 5

Per-console foreground PID for Ctrl-C signal delivery.

    val PM_MAX_CONSOLES = 4
    var fg_pid: [4]int

Waitpid queue: tracks parents blocked waiting for a child.
Each entry stores the waiting parent's thread ID (the IPC
sender), the PID they're waiting for, and whether the slot
is active.

    struct PMWaiter
        active: int
        sender: int
        pid: int

    var waiters: [8]PMWaiter  // 8 = MAX_WAITERS
    var pm_tid = -1

Big-endian message helpers (local to this server).

    pm_msg_read_i32(buf: *byte, off: int) -> int
        ((buf[off] & 0xFF) << 24) | ((buf[off + 1] & 0xFF) << 16) | ((buf[off + 2] & 0xFF) << 8) | (buf[off + 3] & 0xFF)

    pm_msg_write_i32(buf: *byte, off: int, v: int)
        buf[off] = (v >> 24) & 0xFF
        buf[off + 1] = (v >> 16) & 0xFF
        buf[off + 2] = (v >> 8) & 0xFF
        buf[off + 3] = v & 0xFF

Handle a process exit notification from the kernel.
The notification value is the PID of the exited process.
Check if any parent is waiting for this PID and reply to them.
If no waiter, the zombie stays until waitpid is called.

    pm_handle_exit(pid: int)
        if pid <= 0
            return
        if pid >= MAX_PROCESSES
            return
        // Save parent TID and dead main TID before any reaping —
        // both are needed for cleanup fan-out and reap clears the
        // process entry.
        val dead_parent_tid = svc_proc_parent_tid(pid)
        val dead_main_tid = svc_proc_main_tid(pid)
        // Close the dead process's VFS handles (stdin/stdout/stderr etc.)
        // before reaping, so OFT slots are freed for reuse.
        pm_vfs_close_handles(pid)
        // Reclaim any POSIX sockets the process was holding. The inet
        // server tracks sockets by owner_tid, not pid, so we send the
        // dead main_tid. No-op if inet isn't running.
        pm_inet_close_sockets(dead_main_tid)
        // Same for unix sockets — slots tracked by owner-tid in the
        // multi-owner ring; UNIX_CMD_PID_EXIT removes the dead tid
        // from every slot it appears in. No-op if unix isn't running.
        pm_unix_close_sockets(dead_main_tid)
        // Check if a parent is already waiting for this PID
        var i = 0
        while i < MAX_WAITERS
            if waiters[i].active != 0
                if waiters[i].pid == pid
                    // Parent is waiting — reap and reply
                    val exit_code = svc_proc_exit_code(pid)
                    svc_reap_proc(pid)
                    var reply: [5]byte
                    reply[0] = 0
                    pm_msg_write_i32(reply, 1, exit_code)
                    ipc_reply(waiters[i].sender, reply, 5)
                    waiters[i].active = 0
                    return
            i += 1
        // No waiter — zombie stays until parent calls waitpid

        // Reparent any children of the exiting process to init (thread 0).
        // This ensures orphaned processes can still be reaped.
        var j = 1
        while j < MAX_PROCESSES
            if svc_proc_state(j) == PROC_RUNNING
                if svc_proc_parent_tid(j) == dead_main_tid
                    svc_proc_set_parent_tid(j, 0)
            j += 1

        // Notify RS if this was one of its children (a server).
        // RS will restart the dead server from its boot module.
        if rs_tid >= 0
            if dead_parent_tid == rs_tid
                notify_send_to(rs_tid, pid)

Handle PM_WAITPID: parent wants to wait for a child process.
If the child is already a zombie, reap immediately and reply.
If the child is still running, block the parent by deferring
the IPC reply until the child exits.

    pm_handle_waitpid(sender: int, buf: *byte)
        val pid = pm_msg_read_i32(buf, 1)
        if pid <= 0
            var reply: [5]byte
            reply[0] = byte(-1)
            pm_msg_write_i32(reply, 1, 0)
            ipc_reply(sender, reply, 5)
            return
        if pid >= MAX_PROCESSES
            var reply: [5]byte
            reply[0] = byte(-1)
            pm_msg_write_i32(reply, 1, 0)
            ipc_reply(sender, reply, 5)
            return

        val st = svc_proc_state(pid)

        // Already zombie? Reap immediately.
        if st == PROC_ZOMBIE
            val exit_code = svc_proc_exit_code(pid)
            svc_reap_proc(pid)
            var reply: [5]byte
            reply[0] = 0
            pm_msg_write_i32(reply, 1, exit_code)
            ipc_reply(sender, reply, 5)
            return

        // Already free? Error.
        if st == PROC_FREE
            var reply: [5]byte
            reply[0] = byte(-1)
            pm_msg_write_i32(reply, 1, 0)
            ipc_reply(sender, reply, 5)
            return

        // Child still running — defer reply until exit
        var i = 0
        while i < MAX_WAITERS
            if waiters[i].active == 0
                waiters[i].active = 1
                waiters[i].sender = sender
                waiters[i].pid = pid
                // Don't reply now — sender stays blocked
                return
            i += 1

        // Out of waiter slots
        var reply: [5]byte
        reply[0] = byte(-2)
        pm_msg_write_i32(reply, 1, 0)
        ipc_reply(sender, reply, 5)

Handle PM_KILL: kill a process by PID.

    pm_handle_kill(sender: int, buf: *byte)
        val pid = pm_msg_read_i32(buf, 1)
        val result = svc_kill_proc(pid)
        var reply: [1]byte
        if result < 0
            reply[0] = byte(-1)
        else
            reply[0] = 0
        ipc_reply(sender, reply, 1)

Handle PM_SIGNAL: deliver a signal to a process.
Request: [cmd(1), pid(4), sig(1)]
Reply:   [status(1)]

    pm_handle_signal(sender: int, buf: *byte)
        val pid = pm_msg_read_i32(buf, 1)
        val sig = int(buf[5])
        var reply: [1]byte
        if sig == SIG_CHILD
            // Default action: ignore
            reply[0] = 0
        else if sig == SIG_KILL
            svc_kill_proc(pid)
            reply[0] = 0
        else if sig == SIG_TERM
            svc_kill_proc(pid)
            reply[0] = 0
        else if sig == SIG_INT
            svc_kill_proc(pid)
            reply[0] = 0
        else if sig == SIG_PIPE
            svc_kill_proc(pid)
            reply[0] = 0
        else
            reply[0] = byte(-1)
        ipc_reply(sender, reply, 1)

Handle PM_SET_FG: set foreground PID for a console.
Request: [cmd(1), console(1), pid(4)]

    pm_handle_set_fg(sender: int, buf: *byte)
        val console = int(buf[1])
        if console >= 0
            if console < PM_MAX_CONSOLES
                fg_pid[console] = pm_msg_read_i32(buf, 2)
        var reply: [1]byte
        reply[0] = 0
        ipc_reply(sender, reply, 1)

Handle PM_SIGINT: Ctrl-C on a console. Kill foreground process.
Request: [cmd(1), console(1)]

    pm_handle_sigint(sender: int, buf: *byte)
        val console = int(buf[1])
        var reply: [1]byte
        reply[0] = 0
        if console >= 0
            if console < PM_MAX_CONSOLES
                val pid = fg_pid[console]
                if pid >= 0
                    svc_kill_proc(pid)
                    fg_pid[console] = -1
        ipc_reply(sender, reply, 1)

Handle SPAWN: create a new process from a binary path.
Request: [cmd(1), path_len(1), blob_len_lo(1), blob_len_hi(1), num_handles(1), path(N), blob(M), handle_pairs(H*2)]
Reply:   [status(1), pid(4)]

The `blob` is the PM-SPAWN wire-format argv image (see
`build_argv_blob`): a 4-byte argc + 4 pad bytes + the
NUL-separated argv string data. PM parses it and builds a
System V ABI init stack frame in the child's user stack (see
`pm_build_sysv_stack`). The thread name comes from `path`, not
argv.

    pm_handle_spawn(sender: int, buf: *byte)
        pm_handle_spawn_inner(sender, buf, 1)

PM_CMD_SPAWN_SUSP: identical wire format and lifecycle as
PM_CMD_SPAWN, but the child is left suspended after the
reply is sent. The caller is expected to follow up with
PM_CMD_TRANSPLANT_FD (one or more times) and finally
PM_CMD_RESUME to start the child running. Used by the
synthetic transplant test (and, looking ahead, by
arbitrary-kind SCM_RIGHTS-on-fork-style flows).

    pm_handle_spawn_susp(sender: int, buf: *byte)
        pm_handle_spawn_inner(sender, buf, 0)

Shared spawn body. `resume_after != 0` resumes the child
before replying (the original SPAWN behaviour); `0` leaves
the child suspended for follow-up TRANSPLANT_FD/RESUME.

    pm_handle_spawn_inner(sender: int, buf: *byte, resume_after: int)
        val path_len = int(buf[1])
        val blob_len = (int(buf[2]) & 0xFF) | ((int(buf[3]) & 0xFF) << 8)
        val num_handles = int(buf[4])

        val path = string(buf + 5, path_len)

        val proc_pt = svc_vm_create_pt()
        if proc_pt == 0
            var reply: [1]byte
            reply[0] = -1
            ipc_reply(sender, reply, 1)
            return

        val entry = load_tof_to_ptbr(path, proc_pt)
        if entry < 0
            var reply: [1]byte
            reply[0] = -1
            ipc_reply(sender, reply, 1)
            return

        var stack_buf: [4096]byte
        val fsz = pm_build_sysv_stack(buf + 5 + path_len, blob_len,
                                      &stack_buf[0], 4096, PROG_USP)
        if fsz < 0
            var reply: [1]byte
            reply[0] = -1
            ipc_reply(sender, reply, 1)
            return
        val child_sp = PROG_USP - i64(fsz)
        svc_vm_copy_to(proc_pt, u64(child_sp), &stack_buf[0], fsz)

        val pid = svc_create_proc_susp(entry, child_sp, PROG_SSP, path, proc_pt)

        var reply: [5]byte
        if pid < 0
            reply[0] = -1
            ipc_reply(sender, reply, 1)
        else
            svc_grant_posix_range(pid)
            if num_handles > 0
                val sender_pid = svc_get_thread_pid(sender)
                pm_inherit_handles(sender_pid, pid, buf, path_len, blob_len, num_handles)
            if resume_after != 0
                svc_resume_proc(pid)
            reply[0] = 0
            pm_msg_write_i32(reply, 1, pid)
            ipc_reply(sender, reply, 5)

`PM_CMD_EXECVE` (10) — Phase 1 chunk 8.

Replace the calling thread's entire image with one loaded from a new
ELF binary. POSIX-style: same pid, same fd table, same parent. The
old code/data/heap/stack are gone the moment this returns success.

Wire: cmd(1) + path_len(1) + blob_len(2) + pad(1) + path + blob.

Identical to `PM_CMD_SPAWN`'s wire so `pm_build_sysv_stack` can reuse
the spawn argv blob format unchanged. The pad byte (offset 4) is
ignored — execve never inherits handles from a `SCM_RIGHTS`-style
fan-out (POSIX execve preserves the existing fd table by design).

Failure paths reply normally (`reply[0] = -1`, sender resumes at the
old `ipc_send` site and reads the error byte). The success path
takes a different exit: after we've dropped the caller's old VMAs
and pages and rebuilt them around the new image, the old reply
buffer's VA either falls inside the new SysV init stack (typical, on
both arches the new SP and old reply slot are within ~hundreds of
bytes of `PROG_USP`) or onto an unmapped frame. A normal `ipc_reply`
would then either silently corrupt the new stack or fault — neither
acceptable. So we use `svc_execve_finalize` instead, which writes
the new entry PC + new USP into the saved exception frame and wakes
the caller without copying any reply bytes back. The caller's next
`iretq` / `ERET` lands at the new entry with the SysV stack live.

Resource ceilings: the ELF must fit inside PM's heap. With PM's heap
sized around 128 KiB on x86 today, that practically caps user
binaries at ~80 KiB until PM gets a streaming path or its heap is
grown. `LOAD_CROSS_BUF_MAX` upstream is 1 MiB; PM's `malloc` is the
binding constraint here.

    pm_handle_execve(sender: int, buf: *byte)
        val path_len = int(buf[1])
        val blob_len = (int(buf[2]) & 0xFF) | ((int(buf[3]) & 0xFF) << 8)
        val path = string(buf + 5, path_len)

        var reply: [1]byte

Resolve the caller. PM's IPC dispatcher gives us `sender` (the
calling thread); we need the calling process's pid + ptbr for the
image-replacement primitives, and the caller's main_tid (which for
chunk 8 is the same thread, but we route through the API in case the
thread layout grows).

        val sender_pid = svc_get_thread_pid(sender)
        if sender_pid <= 0
            reply[0] = byte(-1)
            ipc_reply(sender, reply, 1)
            return
        val target_ptbr = svc_get_thread_ptbr(sender)
        if target_ptbr == u64(0)
            reply[0] = byte(-1)
            ipc_reply(sender, reply, 1)
            return

Read the main exe file into PM's heap, mirroring `load_tof_to_ptbr`'s
shape so PM and the loader cap user binaries the same way.

        var st: [7]int
        if fs_stat(path, st) < 0
            reply[0] = byte(-1)
            ipc_reply(sender, reply, 1)
            return
        val size = st[4]
        if size <= 0
            reply[0] = byte(-1)
            ipc_reply(sender, reply, 1)
            return

        val fid = fs_open(path)
        if fid < 0
            reply[0] = byte(-1)
            ipc_reply(sender, reply, 1)
            return
        val ebuf = malloc(i64(size))
        if i64(ebuf) <= 0
            fs_close(fid)
            reply[0] = byte(-1)
            ipc_reply(sender, reply, 1)
            return
        var total = 0
        var nr = fs_read(fid, ebuf, size)
        while nr > 0
            total += nr
            if total >= size
                break
            nr = fs_read(fid, ebuf + total, size - total)
        fs_close(fid)
        if total < size
            free(ebuf)
            reply[0] = byte(-1)
            ipc_reply(sender, reply, 1)
            return

Validate the main ELF before touching the caller's address space.
After `svc_proc_replace_image_prep` the caller has no resumable old
image to fall back to, so every check that *could* reject the binary
must run first — and that includes the interpreter load below, since
the interp's path comes from the main exe's PT_INTERP segment.

        if elf64_validate(ebuf, total) != ELF_OK
            free(ebuf)
            reply[0] = byte(-1)
            ipc_reply(sender, reply, 1)
            return

If the binary declares a PT_INTERP segment, validate the interpreter
ELF and keep its file descriptor open for streaming. Static binaries
(no PT_INTERP) fall through this block with `ifid_interp == -1` and
behave exactly as before — same in-PM-heap load, same auxv shape with
`AT_BASE = 0`.

The interp file (typically `ld-musl-<arch>.so.1`) is too big to fit
in PM's heap — at ~900 KiB it exceeds the ~430 KiB heap window
between PM's BSS and its 16-page user-stack region. Streaming the
load into the target address space is the only path that scales:
we read only the EHDR + PHT into a 4 KiB stack buffer (`ihdr_buf`)
for validation + VMA shape, then keep the file open and `fs_seek`
to each PT_LOAD's offset, copying p_filesz bytes through a small
bounce buffer directly into `target_ptbr` via `svc_vm_copy_to`.
The ifid stays open until after VMAs are installed; we close and
finalize entry below.

`ifid_interp == -1` is the no-PT_INTERP case; static binaries
follow the existing in-PM-heap path for the main exe and have no
interp at all.

        var ihdr_buf: [4096]byte
        var ihdr_read = 0
        var ifid_interp = -1
        var interp_base: i64 = i64(0)
        var main_load_offset: i64 = i64(0)

        val interp_idx = pm_find_pt_interp(ebuf)
        if interp_idx >= 0
            val ip_off = int(elf64_ph_offset(ebuf, interp_idx))
            val ip_fsz = int(elf64_ph_filesz(ebuf, interp_idx))
            if ip_off + ip_fsz > total
                free(ebuf)
                reply[0] = byte(-1)
                ipc_reply(sender, reply, 1)
                return
            if ip_fsz <= 1
                free(ebuf)
                reply[0] = byte(-1)
                ipc_reply(sender, reply, 1)
                return

The PT_INTERP segment carries a NUL-terminated path — `p_filesz`
includes the trailing NUL, so the actual string is `ip_fsz - 1`
bytes long. `string(p, n)` copies into a heap-managed string so the
path stays valid even after we eventually `free(ebuf)`.

            val interp_path = string(ebuf + ip_off, ip_fsz - 1)

            var ist: [7]int
            if fs_stat(interp_path, ist) < 0
                free(ebuf)
                reply[0] = byte(-1)
                ipc_reply(sender, reply, 1)
                return
            val isize = ist[4]
            if isize <= 0
                free(ebuf)
                reply[0] = byte(-1)
                ipc_reply(sender, reply, 1)
                return

            ifid_interp = fs_open(interp_path)
            if ifid_interp < 0
                free(ebuf)
                reply[0] = byte(-1)
                ipc_reply(sender, reply, 1)
                return

Read the EHDR + PHT into the stack buffer. 4 KiB covers any
reasonable ELF64 — EHDR is 64 bytes, each program header is 56
bytes, and ld-musl + dhello-style binaries have on the order of
10 PHs. If a binary exceeds this we'd need to enlarge the buffer
or fall back to a heap allocation; today every musl shipping in
slix fits well under it.

            var hr = fs_read(ifid_interp, &ihdr_buf[0], 4096)
            while hr > 0
                ihdr_read += hr
                if ihdr_read >= 4096
                    break
                hr = fs_read(ifid_interp, &ihdr_buf[0] + ihdr_read, 4096 - ihdr_read)

            if ihdr_read < ELF64_EHDR_SIZE
                fs_close(ifid_interp)
                free(ebuf)
                reply[0] = byte(-1)
                ipc_reply(sender, reply, 1)
                return

`elf64_validate` checks magic, class, data, and that the PHT fits
inside `buflen` — which is `ihdr_read` here, the bytes we actually
read (≤ 4096). Since the entire PHT is in `ihdr_buf`, this is the
right validation surface even when the rest of the file is
streamed later.

            if elf64_validate(&ihdr_buf[0], ihdr_read) != ELF_OK
                fs_close(ifid_interp)
                free(ebuf)
                reply[0] = byte(-1)
                ipc_reply(sender, reply, 1)
                return

            interp_base = INTERP_BASE

The presence of PT_INTERP marks the main executable as PIE in
practice — toolchains never emit a PT_INTERP segment for ET_EXEC
non-relocatable binaries. PIE main exes link with their PT_LOAD
vaddrs near 0; we rebase them to `MAIN_EXE_BASE` so they land
inside the user-accessible carve-out. Static binaries (the
`test_execve` chunk-8 path) leave `main_load_offset = 0` and keep
their link-time VAs (slix-prog.ld puts those at 0x60000000+).

            main_load_offset = MAIN_EXE_BASE

Build the SysV init stack into a PM-local buffer up front, while the
caller's old address space is still alive. The auxv carries the
main exe's PHT runtime VA (offset by `main_load_offset` for PIE),
the interp base (0 if no interp), and the main exe's runtime entry
— ld-musl jumps to AT_ENTRY once it finishes its own bootstrap.

        val main_entry = i64(elf64_entry(ebuf)) + main_load_offset
        val main_phnum = elf64_phnum(ebuf)
        val main_phdr_va = pm_compute_at_phdr(ebuf, main_load_offset)

        var stack_buf: [4096]byte
        val fsz = pm_build_sysv_stack_v2(buf + 5 + path_len, blob_len,
                                         &stack_buf[0], 4096, PROG_USP,
                                         main_phdr_va, main_phnum,
                                         interp_base, main_entry)
        if fsz < 0
            if ifid_interp >= 0
                fs_close(ifid_interp)
            free(ebuf)
            reply[0] = byte(-1)
            ipc_reply(sender, reply, 1)
            return

Point of no return. Drop the caller's old VMAs + leaf pages. After
this the page table is empty in the user range; subsequent
`svc_vm_copy_to` calls will allocate-on-write fresh frames as they
go. Failures past this point cannot be reported back to the caller —
the old reply buf is gone — so we simply continue and trust the
syscall layer.

        svc_proc_replace_image_prep(sender_pid)

Install one VMA per `PT_LOAD` so that future demand-paging faults
(stack growth, BSS pages we don't pre-touch) route through
`vma_handle_fault`. Bit-translate ELF p_flags to VMA prot via
`elf64_pf_to_vma_prot` so an R+X text segment doesn't accidentally
get write permission — that's exactly what chunk 7's parser was
shaped to enable.

        val phnum = elf64_phnum(ebuf)
        var i = 0
        while i < phnum
            if elf64_ph_type(ebuf, i) == ELF_PT_LOAD
                val pf = elf64_ph_flags(ebuf, i)
                val prot = elf64_pf_to_vma_prot(pf)
                val vaddr_rt = elf64_ph_vaddr(ebuf, i) + u64(main_load_offset)
                val vstart = elf64_align_down(vaddr_rt, PAGE_SIZE)
                val vend_raw = vaddr_rt + u64(elf64_ph_memsz(ebuf, i))
                val vend = elf64_align_up(vend_raw, PAGE_SIZE)
                svc_vma_create(sender_pid, vstart, vend, prot,
                               VMA_MAP_ANON | VMA_MAP_PRIVATE)
            i += 1

Stack VMA: matches `vma_seed_default_layout`'s shape so a stack-grow
fault at `PROG_USP - n` for any `n` inside this 64 KiB range is
satisfied by `vma_handle_fault`. Hardcoded to PROG_USP - 64 KiB
deliberately — Phase 1 stays on the fixed-stack-region pattern; the
auto-grow VMA is a Phase 2 concern.

        val stack_low = u64(PROG_USP) - u64(0x10000)
        svc_vma_create(sender_pid, stack_low, u64(PROG_USP),
                       VMA_PROT_READ | VMA_PROT_WRITE,
                       VMA_MAP_ANON | VMA_MAP_PRIVATE)

Install one VMA per interp PT_LOAD shifted by `INTERP_BASE`. Same
shape as the main-exe loop above — the interpreter is a PIE built
to load at vaddr 0, so adding `INTERP_BASE` to every `p_vaddr`
slides it into the 1 GiB-aligned region that doesn't collide with
the main exe. The PHT lives in `ihdr_buf` (the streamed-in 4 KiB
header window) since we never read the full file.

        if ifid_interp >= 0
            val ip_phnum_v = elf64_phnum(&ihdr_buf[0])
            var ipi = 0
            while ipi < ip_phnum_v
                if elf64_ph_type(&ihdr_buf[0], ipi) == ELF_PT_LOAD
                    val ipf = elf64_ph_flags(&ihdr_buf[0], ipi)
                    val iprot = elf64_pf_to_vma_prot(ipf)
                    val ipvaddr = elf64_ph_vaddr(&ihdr_buf[0], ipi) + u64(INTERP_BASE)
                    val ipvstart = elf64_align_down(ipvaddr, PAGE_SIZE)
                    val ipvend_raw = ipvaddr + u64(elf64_ph_memsz(&ihdr_buf[0], ipi))
                    val ipvend = elf64_align_up(ipvend_raw, PAGE_SIZE)
                    svc_vma_create(sender_pid, ipvstart, ipvend, iprot,
                                   VMA_MAP_ANON | VMA_MAP_PRIVATE)
                ipi += 1

Copy the new ELF content into the target address space. The shared
loader handles file→memory layout (filesz copy + memsz tail zero-
fill) one PT_LOAD at a time, exactly as it does in
`pm_handle_spawn_inner`. Then load the interp at `INTERP_BASE` if
present, and pick the final entry point — main exe entry for
static binaries, interp entry for dynamic ones.

        val entry = load_elf64_to_ptbr_at_offset(ebuf, total, i64(target_ptbr), main_load_offset)
        free(ebuf)

Stream interp PT_LOAD content into the target address space. The
file is too big to hold in PM's heap (~430 KiB heap vs ~900 KiB
for ld-musl), so for each PT_LOAD we `fs_seek` to its offset and
read p_filesz bytes through a 4 KiB bounce buffer, calling
`svc_vm_copy_to` to land them at `INTERP_BASE + p_vaddr`. Tail
bytes (p_memsz > p_filesz, the .bss portion) get zero-filled the
same way `load_elf64_to_ptbr_at_offset` does for in-memory loads.

        var final_entry: i64 = entry
        if ifid_interp >= 0
            val ip_phnum2 = elf64_phnum(&ihdr_buf[0])
            val ip_link_entry = i64(elf64_entry(&ihdr_buf[0]))
            var ipl = 0
            while ipl < ip_phnum2
                if elf64_ph_type(&ihdr_buf[0], ipl) == ELF_PT_LOAD
                    val p_off = int(elf64_ph_offset(&ihdr_buf[0], ipl))
                    val p_va  = elf64_ph_vaddr(&ihdr_buf[0], ipl) + u64(INTERP_BASE)
                    val p_fsz = int(elf64_ph_filesz(&ihdr_buf[0], ipl))
                    val p_msz = int(elf64_ph_memsz(&ihdr_buf[0], ipl))
                    if p_fsz > 0
                        fs_seek(ifid_interp, p_off, 0)
                        var bounce: [4096]byte
                        var done = 0
                        while done < p_fsz
                            var chunk = p_fsz - done
                            if chunk > 4096
                                chunk = 4096
                            val nr = fs_read(ifid_interp, &bounce[0], chunk)
                            if nr <= 0
                                done = p_fsz
                            else
                                svc_vm_copy_to(target_ptbr, p_va + u64(done), &bounce[0], nr)
                                done += nr
                    if p_msz > p_fsz
                        var zeros: [4096]byte
                        var zdone = p_fsz
                        while zdone < p_msz
                            var zchunk = p_msz - zdone
                            if zchunk > 4096
                                zchunk = 4096
                            svc_vm_copy_to(target_ptbr, p_va + u64(zdone), &zeros[0], zchunk)
                            zdone += zchunk
                ipl += 1
            fs_close(ifid_interp)
            if ip_link_entry > i64(0)
                final_entry = ip_link_entry + INTERP_BASE

        if final_entry <= 0
            // Caller's old image is already gone — there's nothing to
            // resume. Wake them with PC=0 / SP=0 so the very next
            // ring-3 transition #PFs on instruction fetch and the
            // fault path kills the process. Better than leaving them
            // stuck in STATE_SEND_BLOCKED forever waiting for a reply
            // that will never come.
            svc_execve_finalize(sender, i64(0), i64(0))
            return

        val child_sp = i64(PROG_USP) - i64(fsz)
        svc_vm_copy_to(target_ptbr, u64(child_sp), &stack_buf[0], fsz)

Hand control to the new entry point. `svc_execve_finalize` rewrites
the saved PC/USP and wakes the calling thread from
`STATE_SEND_BLOCKED` without writing the reply byte (which would
land in our freshly-built SysV stack). On its next ring-3 transition
the thread runs the new program from a clean state.

        svc_execve_finalize(sender, final_entry, child_sp)

PM_CMD_RESUME: resume a previously SPAWN_SUSP-ed child.
Wire: cmd(1) + pid(4) → status(1).

    pm_handle_resume(sender: int, buf: *byte)
        val pid = pm_msg_read_i32(buf, 1)
        var reply: [1]byte
        if pid <= 0
            reply[0] = byte(-1)
            ipc_reply(sender, reply, 1)
            return
        if pid >= MAX_PROCESSES
            reply[0] = byte(-1)
            ipc_reply(sender, reply, 1)
            return
        if svc_proc_state(pid) != PROC_RUNNING
            // Resume only makes sense on a suspended-but-live process;
            // suspended children show up as PROC_RUNNING because
            // `svc_create_proc_susp` already promoted them past PROC_FREE
            // (the process slot is allocated; it's just the main
            // thread that's parked in suspended state until svc_resume_proc
            // requeues it). We rely on that invariant rather than a
            // separate PROC_SUSPENDED state.
            reply[0] = byte(-1)
            ipc_reply(sender, reply, 1)
            return
        svc_resume_proc(pid)
        reply[0] = 0
        ipc_reply(sender, reply, 1)

PM_CMD_TRANSPLANT_FD: copy a posix_fd_table slot from the
sender's process into a target process's slot. Used to set
up an fd in a SPAWN_SUSP child before resuming it. The
heavy lifting (slot copy + unix server owners-ring fan-out)
happens in the shim's `posix_fd_transplant`; this handler
validates dst_pid is a live process and forwards.

Wire: cmd(1) + dst_pid(2) + src_fd(1) + dst_fd(1) → status(1).

    pm_handle_transplant_fd(sender: int, buf: *byte)
        val dst_pid = (int(buf[1]) & 0xff) | ((int(buf[2]) & 0xff) << 8)
        val src_fd = int(buf[3]) & 0xff
        val dst_fd = int(buf[4]) & 0xff
        var reply: [1]byte
        if dst_pid <= 0
            reply[0] = byte(-1)
            ipc_reply(sender, reply, 1)
            return
        if dst_pid >= MAX_PROCESSES
            reply[0] = byte(-1)
            ipc_reply(sender, reply, 1)
            return
        if svc_proc_state(dst_pid) != PROC_RUNNING
            reply[0] = byte(-1)
            ipc_reply(sender, reply, 1)
            return
        val rc = svc_fd_transplant(sender, src_fd, dst_pid, dst_fd)
        reply[0] = byte(rc & 0xff)
        ipc_reply(sender, reply, 1)

Send handle inheritance request to VFS.

    pm_inherit_handles(parent_pid: int, child_pid: int, buf: *byte, path_len: int, blob_len: int, num_handles: int)
        // Look up VFS port
        val fs_port = port_lookup("fs")
        if fs_port < 0
            return
        // Build INHERIT message: [cmd(1), parent_pid(4), child_pid(4), num(1), pairs(N*2)]
        var msg: [64]byte
        msg[0] = 16  // VFS_CMD_INHERIT
        pm_msg_write_i32(msg, 1, parent_pid)
        pm_msg_write_i32(msg, 5, child_pid)
        msg[9] = byte(num_handles)
        // Copy handle pairs from spawn message
        val pairs_offset = 5 + path_len + blob_len
        var i = 0
        while i < num_handles * 2
            msg[10 + i] = buf[pairs_offset + i]
            i += 1
        var vfs_reply: [1]byte
        ipc_send(fs_port, msg, 10 + num_handles * 2, vfs_reply, 1)

Tell VFS to close all handles for a dead process.

    pm_vfs_close_handles(pid: int)
        val fs_port = port_lookup("fs")
        if fs_port < 0
            return
        var msg: [5]byte
        msg[0] = 17  // VFS_CMD_EXIT
        pm_msg_write_i32(msg, 1, pid)
        var vfs_reply: [1]byte
        ipc_send(fs_port, msg, 5, vfs_reply, 1)

Tell the inet server to reclaim any POSIX sockets a dead process
was holding. Sockets track `owner_tid`, not pid — the inet server
would otherwise leak one slot per socket for every process that
exits without calling close(). The inet port may not be registered
(boot without NIC, test configurations), in which case we silently
skip — no network stack means no leak to clean up.

    pm_inet_close_sockets(dead_tid: int)
        val inet_port = port_lookup("inet")
        if inet_port < 0
            return
        var msg: [5]byte
        msg[0] = 23  // INET_CMD_PID_EXIT
        pm_msg_write_i32(msg, 1, dead_tid)
        var inet_reply: [1]byte
        ipc_send(inet_port, msg, 5, inet_reply, 1)

Tell the unix server to clean up AF_UNIX sockets a dead process
was holding. Mirror of `pm_inet_close_sockets` — sends
`UNIX_CMD_PID_EXIT` with the dead main_tid; the server walks
its slot table, removes the tid from every owners ring it
appears in, and orphan-frees any slot that drops to zero
owners and zero queue references. No-op if the unix server
isn't running.

    pm_unix_close_sockets(dead_tid: int)
        val unix_port = port_lookup("unix")
        if unix_port < 0
            return
        var msg: [5]byte
        msg[0] = 11  // UNIX_CMD_PID_EXIT
        pm_msg_write_i32(msg, 1, dead_tid)
        var unix_reply: [1]byte
        ipc_send(unix_port, msg, 5, unix_reply, 1)

PM server main loop. Uses ipc_recv_notify to wait for either
IPC messages (waitpid/kill from userspace) or notifications
(exit events from the kernel).

    pm_server()
        pm_tid = thread_id()
        svc_register_pm(pm_tid)
        // Init foreground PID table
        var fi = 0
        while fi < PM_MAX_CONSOLES
            fg_pid[fi] = -1
            fi += 1
        val port = port_create()
        if port < 0
            return
        port_register(port, "pm")

        // Signal RS that PM is ready
        rs_ready()

        var buf: [512]byte
        while true
            val result = ipc_recv_notify(port, buf, 512)
            if result == -2
                // Notification: process exited
                val pid = notify_read_self()
                pm_handle_exit(pid)
            else if result >= 0
                // IPC message
                val sender = result
                val cmd = int(buf[0])
                if cmd == PM_CMD_WAITPID
                    pm_handle_waitpid(sender, buf)
                else if cmd == PM_CMD_KILL
                    pm_handle_kill(sender, buf)
                else if cmd == PM_CMD_SPAWN
                    pm_handle_spawn(sender, buf)
                else if cmd == PM_CMD_SIGNAL
                    pm_handle_signal(sender, buf)
                else if cmd == PM_CMD_SET_FG
                    pm_handle_set_fg(sender, buf)
                else if cmd == PM_CMD_SIGINT
                    pm_handle_sigint(sender, buf)
                else if cmd == PM_CMD_SPAWN_SUSP
                    pm_handle_spawn_susp(sender, buf)
                else if cmd == PM_CMD_TRANSPLANT_FD
                    pm_handle_transplant_fd(sender, buf)
                else if cmd == PM_CMD_RESUME
                    pm_handle_resume(sender, buf)
                else if cmd == PM_CMD_EXECVE
                    pm_handle_execve(sender, buf)
                else
                    // Unknown command
                    var reply: [1]byte
                    reply[0] = byte(-1)
                    ipc_reply(sender, reply, 1)

Client API — called by nsh and other programs to interact
with PM via IPC.

    pm_waitpid(pid: int) -> int
        val port = port_lookup("pm")
        if port < 0
            return -1
        var msg: [5]byte
        msg[0] = byte(PM_CMD_WAITPID)
        pm_msg_write_i32(msg, 1, pid)
        var reply: [5]byte
        ipc_send(port, msg, 5, reply, 5)
        if reply[0] != 0
            return -1
        pm_msg_read_i32(reply, 1)

Build the PM-SPAWN wire-format argv blob. Mirror of
ulib.build_argv_blob; see that one for the format spec.

    pm_build_argv_blob(argv: *string, argc: int, blob: *byte, cap: int) -> int
        if 8 > cap
            return -1
        blob[0] = byte(argc & 0xFF)
        blob[1] = byte((argc >> 8) & 0xFF)
        blob[2] = byte((argc >> 16) & 0xFF)
        blob[3] = byte((argc >> 24) & 0xFF)
        blob[4] = 0
        blob[5] = 0
        blob[6] = 0
        blob[7] = 0
        var pos = 8
        var i = 0
        while i < argc
            val arg = argv[i]
            val alen = len(arg)
            if pos + alen + 1 > cap
                return -1
            val ap = *byte(arg)
            var j = 0
            while j < alen
                blob[pos + j] = ap[j]
                j += 1
            blob[pos + alen] = 0
            pos = pos + alen + 1
            i += 1
        pos

Little-endian 64-bit writer used by the rich-auxv stack builder
below. The argc / argv / envp / auxv slots are all 64-bit
little-endian per System V ABI; `pm_msg_write_i32` is big-endian
(network order, used for IPC payloads) and `pm_build_sysv_stack`
inlines its own LE bytes per slot, so a dedicated helper here
keeps the auxv code readable without a sea of shift-and-mask
fragments.

    pm_write_i64_le(p: *byte, off: int, v: i64)
        p[off]     = byte(v & i64(0xFF))
        p[off + 1] = byte((v >> 8) & i64(0xFF))
        p[off + 2] = byte((v >> 16) & i64(0xFF))
        p[off + 3] = byte((v >> 24) & i64(0xFF))
        p[off + 4] = byte((v >> 32) & i64(0xFF))
        p[off + 5] = byte((v >> 40) & i64(0xFF))
        p[off + 6] = byte((v >> 48) & i64(0xFF))
        p[off + 7] = byte((v >> 56) & i64(0xFF))

Find the index of a PT_INTERP segment in `buf`, or -1 if the
binary doesn't declare one. Static binaries (everything currently
built by `oskit/arch/*/build_prog.sh`) return -1 here and PM takes
the no-interp path. Dynamically-linked binaries — those linked with
`-dynamic-linker /lib/ld-musl-*.so.1` — return the segment index
that PM uses to extract the interpreter's path.

    pm_find_pt_interp(buf: *byte) -> int
        val phnum = elf64_phnum(buf)
        var i = 0
        while i < phnum
            if elf64_ph_type(buf, i) == ELF_PT_INTERP
                return i
            i += 1
        -1

`pm_compute_at_phdr` finds the runtime VA of the program header
table by walking PT_LOAD segments for the one whose `[p_offset,
p_offset+p_filesz)` range covers `[e_phoff, e_phoff+phnum*phentsize)`.
That segment's `p_vaddr + (e_phoff - p_offset)` is where the PHT
landed in memory; ld-musl reads it as `AT_PHDR` to bootstrap its
own `dl_phdr_info` walk without re-opening the binary.

`load_offset` mirrors the offset passed to
`load_elf64_to_ptbr_at_offset`: 0 for the main exe (link-time VAs
honored), `INTERP_BASE` for the interpreter (PIE slid by 1 GiB).
Returns 0 when no PT_LOAD covers the PHT — a malformed-but-loadable
binary; ld-musl falls back to other discovery paths in that case.

    pm_compute_at_phdr(buf: *byte, load_offset: i64) -> i64
        val phoff = elf64_phoff(buf)
        val phnum = elf64_phnum(buf)
        val phentsize = elf64_phentsize(buf)
        val pht_end = phoff + i64(phnum) * i64(phentsize)
        var i = 0
        while i < phnum
            if elf64_ph_type(buf, i) == ELF_PT_LOAD
                val p_off = elf64_ph_offset(buf, i)
                val p_fsz = elf64_ph_filesz(buf, i)
                if p_off <= phoff
                    if p_off + p_fsz >= pht_end
                        return i64(elf64_ph_vaddr(buf, i)) + (phoff - p_off) + load_offset
            i += 1
        i64(0)

`pm_build_sysv_stack_v2` is the rich-auxv counterpart to
`pm_build_sysv_stack`. Identical argv/envp wire-in and frame-shape
contract, but the auxv now carries the eleven entries musl's
`__init_libc` reads on aarch64 + x86_64: AT_PHDR, AT_PHENT,
AT_PHNUM, AT_PAGESZ, AT_BASE, AT_ENTRY, AT_PLATFORM, AT_HWCAP,
AT_SECURE, AT_RANDOM, plus the AT_NULL terminator.

`AT_ENTRY` is the **main exe's** e_entry, *not* the interpreter's —
the interp jumps to it once dynamic relocations finish. `AT_BASE`
is `interp_base` (i.e. `INTERP_BASE`) when the binary has a
PT_INTERP and PM loaded ld-musl into that region; 0 otherwise so
musl knows it's not running under an interpreter and `__libc_start_main`
goes straight into bootstrap mode. `AT_PHDR` / `AT_PHENT` / `AT_PHNUM`
describe the *main exe*'s PHT regardless of interp presence —
ld-musl reads its own headers from `r_brk`-style discovery.
`AT_PLATFORM` points at a NUL-terminated copy of
`oskit.arch.PLATFORM_NAME` ("aarch64" or "x86_64"); ld-musl uses
this to pick HWCAP-gated string routines. `AT_RANDOM` points at
16 bytes that musl uses for stack-cookie + ASLR-input seeding;
we currently fill them with `0xA5` (Phase 2 will route real
randomness through here).

Frame layout:

```
+0                          argc (i64 LE)
+8                          argv[0..argc-1] argv[NULL]
+8 + s_argv                 envp[NULL]
+8 + s_argv + s_envp        auxv (11 pairs * 16 = 176 bytes)
... + s_auxv                AT_RANDOM bytes (16)
... + s_random              platform string + NUL (padded to 8)
... + s_platform            argv string data
                            aligned to 16
```

Returns the frame size on success, -1 if the frame doesn't fit
in `cap` or the source blob is malformed.

    pm_build_sysv_stack_v2(src: *byte, src_len: int, out: *byte, cap: int,
                           child_sp_top: i64, main_phdr_va: i64,
                           main_phnum: int, interp_base: i64,
                           main_entry: i64) -> int
        var argc = 0
        if src_len >= 4
            argc = (int(src[0]) & 0xFF) | ((int(src[1]) & 0xFF) << 8) | ((int(src[2]) & 0xFF) << 16) | ((int(src[3]) & 0xFF) << 24)
        if argc < 0
            return -1

        val src_str_off = 8

        var s_total = 0
        if argc > 0
            if src_str_off > src_len
                return -1
            var k = 0
            while k < argc
                var slen = 0
                while src[src_str_off + s_total + slen] != 0
                    slen += 1
                    if src_str_off + s_total + slen >= src_len
                        return -1
                s_total += slen + 1
                k += 1

        val s_argv = (argc + 1) * 8
        val s_envp = 1 * 8
        val s_auxv = 11 * 16             // 10 entries + AT_NULL terminator
        val s_random = 16

        val plat_len = len(PLATFORM_NAME)
        val s_platform = ((plat_len + 1 + 7) / 8) * 8

        var frame_size = 8 + s_argv + s_envp + s_auxv + s_random + s_platform + s_total
        frame_size = ((frame_size + 15) / 16) * 16

        if frame_size > cap
            return -1

        var ci = 0
        while ci < frame_size
            out[ci] = 0
            ci += 1

        val child_sp = child_sp_top - i64(frame_size)

        val argc_off     = 0
        val argv_off     = 8
        val envp_off     = argv_off + s_argv
        val auxv_off     = envp_off + s_envp
        val random_off   = auxv_off + s_auxv
        val platform_off = random_off + s_random
        val str_off      = platform_off + s_platform

        val random_va   = child_sp + i64(random_off)
        val platform_va = child_sp + i64(platform_off)

        pm_write_i64_le(out, argc_off, i64(argc))

        var buf_sp = 0
        var dst_sp = str_off
        var ai = 0
        while ai < argc
            var slen = 0
            while src[src_str_off + buf_sp + slen] != 0
                slen += 1
            var j = 0
            while j <= slen
                out[dst_sp + j] = src[src_str_off + buf_sp + j]
                j += 1
            pm_write_i64_le(out, argv_off + ai * 8, child_sp + i64(dst_sp))
            dst_sp += slen + 1
            buf_sp += slen + 1
            ai += 1

Auxv entries — order doesn't matter to musl, but keep AT_NULL
last. The buffer was zero-cleared above, so the AT_NULL terminator
pair (16 bytes after the last entry) doesn't need explicit writes.

        var ax = auxv_off
        pm_write_i64_le(out, ax,     i64(AT_PHDR))
        pm_write_i64_le(out, ax + 8, main_phdr_va)
        ax += 16
        pm_write_i64_le(out, ax,     i64(AT_PHENT))
        pm_write_i64_le(out, ax + 8, i64(ELF64_PHDR_SIZE))
        ax += 16
        pm_write_i64_le(out, ax,     i64(AT_PHNUM))
        pm_write_i64_le(out, ax + 8, i64(main_phnum))
        ax += 16
        pm_write_i64_le(out, ax,     i64(AT_PAGESZ))
        pm_write_i64_le(out, ax + 8, i64(4096))
        ax += 16
        pm_write_i64_le(out, ax,     i64(AT_BASE))
        pm_write_i64_le(out, ax + 8, interp_base)
        ax += 16
        pm_write_i64_le(out, ax,     i64(AT_ENTRY))
        pm_write_i64_le(out, ax + 8, main_entry)
        ax += 16
        pm_write_i64_le(out, ax,     i64(AT_PLATFORM))
        pm_write_i64_le(out, ax + 8, platform_va)
        ax += 16
        pm_write_i64_le(out, ax,     i64(AT_HWCAP))
        pm_write_i64_le(out, ax + 8, i64(0))
        ax += 16
        pm_write_i64_le(out, ax,     i64(AT_SECURE))
        pm_write_i64_le(out, ax + 8, i64(0))
        ax += 16
        pm_write_i64_le(out, ax,     i64(AT_RANDOM))
        pm_write_i64_le(out, ax + 8, random_va)

AT_RANDOM payload: 16 bytes of placeholder entropy. musl xors this
into its stack cookie and uses some bytes for `arc4random` seeding;
a deterministic value works for bring-up but Phase 2 must wire a
real RNG through here before any security-sensitive code runs.

        var r = 0
        while r < 16
            out[random_off + r] = byte(0xA5)
            r += 1

        val plat_p = *byte(PLATFORM_NAME)
        var pi = 0
        while pi < plat_len
            out[platform_off + pi] = plat_p[pi]
            pi += 1

        frame_size

Build a System V Application Binary Interface process initialization
stack frame in `out`. Layout matches what musl's / NetBSD's / glibc's
`_start` expects — argc at the bottom, then argv pointer array (inc
NULL terminator), then envp pointer array (inc NULL terminator),
then auxv (just AT_NULL pair today), then the string data argv
points into. PM copies the whole frame into the new process's stack
region and hands the bottom address to `svc_create_proc_susp` as
the initial SP_EL0 / RSP / r7.

`src` is the client-side wire-format argv blob (see
`build_argv_blob`): `[argc(4) pad(4) arg0\0 arg1\0 ...]`.

`child_sp_top` is the top of the new process's user stack region
(PROG_USP). `frame_size` is rounded up to a 16-byte boundary and
returned; the initial SP the caller passes to create_proc_susp
is `child_sp_top - frame_size`.

Returns the frame size on success, -1 if the frame wouldn't fit
in `cap` or the blob is malformed.

    pm_build_sysv_stack(src: *byte, src_len: int, out: *byte, cap: int, child_sp_top: i64) -> int
        // Parse argc from the first 4 bytes of the source blob (i32 LE).
        // blob_len==0 → no args, build an empty argv / envp / auxv frame.
        var argc = 0
        if src_len >= 4
            argc = (int(src[0]) & 0xFF) | ((int(src[1]) & 0xFF) << 8) | ((int(src[2]) & 0xFF) << 16) | ((int(src[3]) & 0xFF) << 24)
        if argc < 0
            return -1

        // Source string data starts right after the 8-byte header
        // (argc(4) + 4 bytes pad).
        val src_str_off = 8

        // Walk the string area to sum up total bytes (argv[i] + NUL each).
        var s_total = 0
        if argc > 0
            if src_str_off > src_len
                return -1
            var k = 0
            while k < argc
                var slen = 0
                while src[src_str_off + s_total + slen] != 0
                    slen += 1
                    if src_str_off + s_total + slen >= src_len
                        return -1
                s_total += slen + 1
                k += 1

        // Frame size: argc(8) + argv+NULL((argc+1)*8) + envp NULL(8)
        //             + auxv (AT_PAGESZ + AT_NULL = 2 pairs * 16 bytes)
        //             + string bytes.
        // AT_PAGESZ=4096 is required by musl's __init_libc (libc.page_size
        // is read from auxv); without it, malloc's page-alignment math
        // computes `n & (PAGE_SIZE-1)` as `n & -1` and zeroes everything,
        // making malloc fall through to the unimplemented mmap path.
        val s_argv = (argc + 1) * 8
        val s_envp = 1 * 8
        val s_auxv = 4 * 8     // AT_PAGESZ pair + AT_NULL pair
        var frame_size = 8 + s_argv + s_envp + s_auxv + s_total

        // 16-byte align (SysV ABI / AArch64 SP alignment requirement).
        frame_size = ((frame_size + 15) / 16) * 16

        if frame_size > cap
            return -1

        // Zero the frame buffer up front so pointer slots, NULL
        // terminators, AT_NULL pair and any alignment padding are
        // all zero without extra writes below.
        var ci = 0
        while ci < frame_size
            out[ci] = 0
            ci += 1

        // Where the frame will land in the child's address space.
        val child_sp = child_sp_top - i64(frame_size)

        // argc (i64 LE) at offset 0.
        out[0] = byte(argc & 0xFF)
        out[1] = byte((argc >> 8) & 0xFF)
        out[2] = byte((argc >> 16) & 0xFF)
        out[3] = byte((argc >> 24) & 0xFF)
        // bytes 4..7 already zero.

        // String data lands after argc + argv + envp + auxv.
        val dst_str_off = 8 + s_argv + s_envp + s_auxv
        var buf_sp = 0
        var dst_sp = dst_str_off
        var ai = 0
        while ai < argc
            var slen = 0
            while src[src_str_off + buf_sp + slen] != 0
                slen += 1
            // Copy string + NUL into the output buffer.
            var j = 0
            while j <= slen
                out[dst_sp + j] = src[src_str_off + buf_sp + j]
                j += 1
            // Write argv[ai] = child VA of this string (child_sp + dst_sp).
            val ptr = child_sp + i64(dst_sp)
            val pbase = 8 + ai * 8
            out[pbase]     = byte(ptr & i64(0xFF))
            out[pbase + 1] = byte((ptr >> 8) & i64(0xFF))
            out[pbase + 2] = byte((ptr >> 16) & i64(0xFF))
            out[pbase + 3] = byte((ptr >> 24) & i64(0xFF))
            out[pbase + 4] = byte((ptr >> 32) & i64(0xFF))
            out[pbase + 5] = byte((ptr >> 40) & i64(0xFF))
            out[pbase + 6] = byte((ptr >> 48) & i64(0xFF))
            out[pbase + 7] = byte((ptr >> 56) & i64(0xFF))
            dst_sp += slen + 1
            buf_sp += slen + 1
            ai += 1

        // argv NULL (at pbase = 8 + argc*8), envp NULL (at 8 + s_argv),
        // AT_NULL pair (at 8 + s_argv + s_envp + 16), and alignment padding
        // are all already zero from the clear above.

        // Write the AT_PAGESZ auxv entry: type=6 at offset 0, value=4096
        // at offset 8 of the auxv region. The AT_NULL terminator pair
        // (16 zero bytes after that) was already cleared.
        val auxv_off = 8 + s_argv + s_envp
        out[auxv_off]      = byte(6)        // AT_PAGESZ = 6
        // bytes auxv_off+1..7 are zero (high bytes of i64 type)
        out[auxv_off + 8]  = byte(0)        // 4096 little-endian:
        out[auxv_off + 9]  = byte(0x10)     //   0x00 0x10 0x00 0x00...
        // bytes auxv_off+10..15 are zero (high bytes of i64 value)

        frame_size

    pm_spawn(path: string, argv: *string, argc: int) -> int
        pm_spawn_handles_inner(path, argv, argc, *byte(0), 0, byte(PM_CMD_SPAWN))

    pm_spawn_handles(path: string, argv: *string, argc: int, handle_pairs: *byte, num_handles: int) -> int
        pm_spawn_handles_inner(path, argv, argc, handle_pairs, num_handles, byte(PM_CMD_SPAWN))

`pm_spawn_susp` shares wire format with `pm_spawn`; the
only delta is the cmd byte. The child is left suspended;
caller follows up with `pm_transplant_fd` (one or more
times) and `pm_resume` to start the child.

    pm_spawn_susp(path: string, argv: *string, argc: int) -> int
        pm_spawn_handles_inner(path, argv, argc, *byte(0), 0, byte(PM_CMD_SPAWN_SUSP))

    pm_spawn_handles_inner(path: string, argv: *string, argc: int,
                           handle_pairs: *byte, num_handles: int, cmd: byte) -> int
        val port = port_lookup("pm")
        if port < 0
            return -1
        val plen = len(path)
        var msg: [512]byte
        val blob_off = 5 + plen
        val cap = 512 - blob_off - num_handles * 2
        val blob_len = pm_build_argv_blob(argv, argc, &msg[blob_off], cap)
        if blob_len < 0
            return -1
        msg[0] = cmd
        msg[1] = byte(plen)
        msg[2] = byte(blob_len & 0xFF)
        msg[3] = byte((blob_len >> 8) & 0xFF)
        msg[4] = byte(num_handles)
        val pp = *byte(path)
        var i = 0
        while i < plen
            msg[5 + i] = pp[i]
            i += 1
        i = 0
        while i < num_handles * 2
            msg[blob_off + blob_len + i] = handle_pairs[i]
            i += 1
        var reply: [5]byte
        ipc_send(port, msg, blob_off + blob_len + num_handles * 2, reply, 5)
        if reply[0] != 0
            return -1
        pm_msg_read_i32(reply, 1)

`pm_transplant_fd` copies one of the caller's posix_fd_table
slots into a destination process's slot. dst_fd must be
unused in the destination's table; the caller must close
any prior occupant first.

    pm_transplant_fd(dst_pid: int, src_fd: int, dst_fd: int) -> int
        val port = port_lookup("pm")
        if port < 0
            return -1
        var msg: [5]byte
        msg[0] = byte(PM_CMD_TRANSPLANT_FD)
        msg[1] = byte(dst_pid & 0xff)
        msg[2] = byte((dst_pid >> 8) & 0xff)
        msg[3] = byte(src_fd & 0xff)
        msg[4] = byte(dst_fd & 0xff)
        var reply: [1]byte
        ipc_send(port, msg, 5, reply, 1)
        var rc = int(reply[0])
        if rc >= 128
            rc = rc - 256
        rc

`pm_resume` resumes a previously SPAWN_SUSP-ed child.

    pm_resume(pid: int) -> int
        val port = port_lookup("pm")
        if port < 0
            return -1
        var msg: [5]byte
        msg[0] = byte(PM_CMD_RESUME)
        pm_msg_write_i32(msg, 1, pid)
        var reply: [1]byte
        ipc_send(port, msg, 5, reply, 1)
        int(reply[0])

    pm_kill(pid: int) -> int
        val port = port_lookup("pm")
        if port < 0
            return -1
        var msg: [5]byte
        msg[0] = byte(PM_CMD_KILL)
        pm_msg_write_i32(msg, 1, pid)
        var reply: [1]byte
        ipc_send(port, msg, 5, reply, 1)
        int(reply[0])
